> The bootrom tell me that I have 2 GB Physical Memory when I issue "sh
> memory".
> Then when I boot up the server I see something similiar to this:
>
> Pyshical Memory 2048Mb
> Available Memory 983Mb
>
> Where did 1 GB memory go? Top report that system got 983 available
> memory.
> vmstat -P claims I have 2GB and I can see that 1032MB memory seem to
> be assigned to a type called "gh" and the rest 983 is assigned to
> "managed".
>
> What can I do and how can I see what the lost 1GB RAM is used for?
>
> Thanks
> Stefan Wold - Song Networks

Stefan,

Use the command : /sbin/sysconfig -q vm gh_chunks
You should see gh_chunks=256  --> 256 * 4MB = 1 GB
From manpage sys_attrs_vm:
  gh_chunks
      Number of 4-MB chunks of memory reserved at boot time for shared
memory
      use. This memory cannot be used for any other purpose, nor can it be
      returned to the system or reclaimed when not being used. On NUMA-aware
      systems (GS80, GS160, and GS320), the gh_chunks attribute affects only
      the first Resource Affinity Domain (RAD). See the entry for
      rad_gh_regions for more information.

If you do not need gh_chunks, remove the entry from /etc/sysconfigtab and
reboot.

Johan.




