In a previous life I used to do Tru64/Oracle performance tuning. 
There never has been and never will be a one-size-fits-all
configuration,  especially for Tru64/Alpha-based Oracle (i.e. serious)
database systems.  This is not Microsoft Access on a Windows 98 PC. 
You need to understand everything about your intended workload (which
is in turn derived from your business model and goals), then do a
system design.  You have literally dozens of combinations from which
to choose if you combine the choices of filesystem (or none), RAID
levels in the storage, backup/restore strategies, database physical
design and logical design, uptime requirements, performance
requirements, allowable downtimes, capabilities of support personnel
(will they call 911 when they don't see the database disks in the
output of a "df" shell command?), and on and on and on.

If this isn't going to be a production system that's required for
making money (or keeping jobs!) then you can try just about anything
and change it later.  If this is the case, use AdvFS-based datafiles
and RAID5 at the hardware-controller level.  That's as good a place to
start as any, I suppose.

I'll look back here in a couple of days to see if you've provided any
more detail WRT the above concerns, then make further recommendations.
 In the meantime you may want to get a systems analyst/DB designer
into your shop to guide you in a non-platform-specific manner, to a
logical design that will accomplish what you want.

QH


