It is exceptionally rare (outside of my experience) for the SCSI
CAM driver to report a hard error with a disk, when there isn't
actually a problem doing I/O to that disk.  The fact that writes
to the disk are getting errors is an indication of something
wrong.  You really need to look at the full event log listing
to see what the problem is.

Lots of things can cause SCSI disks to fail I/O requests.  Bad
media, bad electronics, bad commands, illegal commands, etc.

>
>Is vdump my only (or even _a_) solution?  Damn. Damn. Damn.

Fixing the disk might also be a solution, but you need to know
what the real problem is.  Something I saw once, was a file
system on a partition that extended beyond the end of the
disk.  At the time, AdvFS didn't do a good job of checking
the boundaries of the disk.  When it was initially created,
nothing had tried to write towards the end, but once it did
the SCSI driver started complaining of illegal parameters on
the commands; LBA addresses beyond the end of the disk.

While such an error would be unlikely on a recently created
file system (I think AdvFS checks the end of the disk now),
it is one of example of how reads can succeed and writes can
fail.

